{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And Moses sent to call, etc.</b> He acted wisely in this, to speak with them in private, not at the time of the assembly — perhaps he might persuade their minds, one to one. Also, by the very act of showing them such importance, that he considered them worthy to speak to them — aside from those who had gathered — their hearts might soften to lend an ear. And Scripture was precise in saying <b>And Moses sent to call</b>, etc., for there was no need to mention Moses, since he could rely on his mention in the preceding verse. Rather, it comes to point out who the sender is — Moses, king of Israel and prophet of HaShem — calling two ordinary men; perhaps through this they would come to their senses and incline their ear toward him." "<b>We will not go up, etc. Is it a small thing, etc.</b> Their saying <b>we will not go up</b> — according to what I explained, that Moses acted wisely to elevate them by speaking with them in private, they said: We have no desire for this elevation whereby you consider us important enough to send for us. They also intended by this statement to say that even though this sending was for their benefit, to confer upon them some honor and elevation, nevertheless <b>we will not go up</b> — they reject that elevation which would come to them from Moses." "And they gave a reason for their words, and said <b>Is it a small thing that you have brought us up, etc.</b> — meaning, is this thing you did for us, that you brought us up, a small thing? That is to say, you made the matter for us in the manner of elevation and honor in the exodus from Egypt, yet the end of that elevation was a descent from a land flowing with milk and honey where we were, to put us to death in the wilderness. If so, your elevations are nothing but degradations and descents. And they said <b>that you lord it over us, etc.</b> — meaning, your motive in all these matters is nothing but to exercise dominion, not to benefit us. Also in this sending, that you send for us, you intend not for our good but to exercise dominion, which is what it says: <b>also you lord it over us.</b> They also intended in the doubling of <b>you lord it over us / also you lord it over us</b> to say that the dominion of Moses is doubled and redoubled beyond the authority that had been over them before they left Egypt, and this is the reading of the words: that you exercise authority over us with a multiplication of authorities, which is what it says: <b>also you lord it over us.</b> Or perhaps they also hinted to him that he wishes to prolong the stay in the wilderness in order to extend his own authority, according to what they of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 5:23) that the kingship of Moses endured only in the wilderness, and therefore they said <b>also you lord it over us.</b>"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:10:08.407811188Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}